    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight mb-6">IVF in India: An Honest Guide for Couples Considering Treatment Abroad</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg mb-8">What IVF in India actually costs, what success rates mean, what to look for in a clinic, and how to plan the journey. Written honestly for couples who've already been through a lot.</p>
    <img src="../../src/images/blog-ivf-fertility-featured.png" alt="Fertility specialist discussing IVF treatment options with a couple in India" class="w-full rounded-2xl shadow mb-10">
    <article class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-10">
<p>If you're reading this, you've probably already done a cycle — maybe two. You've learned the language of follicle counts and trigger shots and two-week waits. You know what a failed transfer feels like.</p>
<p>And now you're doing the maths. Because the maths eventually forces the question: is there another way?</p>
<p>India is where many couples find that other way. Not because it's a compromise — but because the care is genuinely good, the cost is a fraction of what you've been paying, and the waiting lists that exist in the UK, Canada, and Australia simply don't exist here.</p>
<h2>What's Available</h2>
<p>Every mainstream fertility treatment is available at India's accredited clinics:</p>
<ul><li><strong>IVF</strong> — ovarian stimulation, egg retrieval, fertilisation, embryo transfer</li><li><strong>ICSI</strong> — single sperm injection, used for male factor infertility; included in most Indian IVF packages</li><li><strong>Frozen Embryo Transfer (FET)</strong> — transfer of embryos from a previous cycle, far cheaper than a full fresh cycle</li><li><strong>Donor Egg IVF</strong> — available and regulated under India's ART Act 2021; donors are screened for genetics, infectious disease, and hormonal profile</li><li><strong>PGT-A / PGD</strong> — genetic testing of embryos before transfer, available at leading centres</li><li><strong>Sperm donation</strong> — available at regulated sperm banks</li></ul>
<p>One important note on <strong>surrogacy</strong>: India's 2021 ART Act restricted commercial surrogacy. Altruistic surrogacy involving a close relative remains possible under specific legal conditions. If surrogacy is relevant to your situation, take legal advice before travelling.</p>
<h2>What It Costs</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-slate-200 py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Treatment</th><th class="border-b border-slate-200 py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">USA</th><th class="border-b border-slate-200 py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">UK (Private)</th><th class="border-b border-slate-200 py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">India</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">IVF — one cycle, own eggs</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$15,000–$25,000</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">£5,000–£8,000</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$2,000–$4,000</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">ICSI (added to IVF)</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$1,500–$3,000 extra</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">£1,000–£2,000 extra</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$300–$600 extra</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">Frozen Embryo Transfer</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$3,000–$6,000</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">£2,000–£3,500</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$500–$1,200</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">Donor Egg IVF</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$25,000–$45,000</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">£8,000–£15,000</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$3,500–$6,000</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">PGT-A per embryo</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$300–$600</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">£200–£400</td><td class="border-b border-slate-100 py-3 pr-4">$80–$150</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><em>Medications are charged separately and vary by protocol. <a class="text-teal-700 font-medium underline" href="https://forms.gle/Snkvg8H7byeD8cXj7">Request a personalised estimate from Sano →</a></em></p>
<p>Even budgeting for two or three cycles in India, most couples spend less than a single cycle at home.</p>
<h2>Understanding Success Rates — Read This Before You Trust Any Number</h2>
<p>This matters more than most clinics want you to know.</p>
<p>The figure that actually counts is <strong>live birth rate per embryo transfer</strong> — the proportion of transfers that result in a baby brought home. This is different from — and always lower than — "clinical pregnancy rate" (a heartbeat on scan) or "positive test rate."</p>
<p>India's leading accredited clinics report live birth rates per transfer of:</p>
<ul><li><strong>40–50%</strong> for women under 35, own eggs</li><li><strong>30–40%</strong> for women aged 35–40</li><li><strong>15–25%</strong> for women over 40, own eggs</li><li><strong>50–65%</strong> for donor egg cycles</li></ul>
<p>These are comparable to the best fertility centres in Europe and North America. The difference is not outcomes — it's cost.</p>
<p>When evaluating any clinic, ask specifically for their live birth rate per embryo transfer, for your age group and diagnosis. A clinic that won't share this number is not a clinic to trust.</p>
<h2>What to Look For in a Clinic</h2>
<p><strong>Registered and accredited.</strong> The clinic must be registered under India's ART Act 2021. NABH accreditation is the additional quality marker to look for.</p>
<p><strong>In-house embryology laboratory.</strong> The quality of the lab — the embryologist's skill, temperature stability, CO₂ levels, equipment — has more impact on outcomes than almost any other factor. The lab should be on-site, not shared.</p>
<p><strong>Same doctor throughout your cycle.</strong> For international patients especially, being rotated between whoever is available that day is not acceptable. Ask explicitly how continuity is managed.</p>
<p><strong>Language support.</strong> Medication instructions, protocol changes, and results conversations are clinical matters, not administrative ones. Miscommunication here has consequences. <a class="text-teal-700 font-medium underline" href="https://www.sanohealth.co.in">Sano Healthcare and Tourism</a> provides multilingual support in 120+ languages throughout your treatment.</p>
<h2>The Journey, Practically</h2>
<p>A fresh IVF cycle from the start of stimulation to embryo transfer takes <strong>14–20 days</strong>. Add a few days for initial consultations and baseline investigations — plan for a <strong>3–4 week stay</strong> for a complete fresh cycle.</p>
<p>Some clinics allow early stimulation monitoring to be done at home, with results shared remotely, then travel to India for egg retrieval and transfer. Ask about this — it can shorten your time in India significantly.</p>
<p>The male partner typically only needs to be in India for egg retrieval day. Logistics for both partners should be discussed with Sano's team during planning.</p>
<p>If you have frozen embryos from your first cycle, a future FET requires only a short return visit — far less travel, far less cost.</p>
<p>After you're home, Sano coordinates remote follow-up with your fertility specialist: two-week test support, early scan guidance, and ongoing contact until you're back under your home doctor's care.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3><strong>How many cycles should we budget for?</strong></h3>
<p>Most specialists suggest planning for two to three if the first doesn't succeed — cumulative success rates are significantly higher than a single cycle. Even across three cycles in India, the total cost usually stays below a single cycle in the US or UK.</p>
<h3><strong>Can we bring frozen embryos from home?</strong></h3>
<p>It's possible but legally and logistically complex. It needs advance coordination between your home clinic, the Indian clinic, and a specialist medical transport provider. Raise this early if it's relevant.</p>
<h3><strong>What are the legal requirements for donor egg IVF?</strong></h3>
<p>Available to married couples at registered ART clinics under India's 2021 ART Act. The clinic verifies documentation. Unmarried couples or same-sex couples should seek specific legal advice on eligibility before travelling.</p>
<h3><strong>Is there an age limit for IVF in India?</strong></h3>
<p>Most clinics follow guidelines recommending IVF up to age 50 for the female partner, with donor eggs where appropriate. Individual clinic policies may vary.</p>
<h2>One Last Thing</h2>
<p>Fertility treatment is one of the most personal decisions a couple makes. The inquiry process at Sano is designed to be honest, not transactional — you share your history, you hear what's realistic for your situation, and you decide from there. No pressure.</p>
